    "verdict": {
      "enso_predicts_winter_nao": false,
      "summary": "Very strong El Nino winters (n=3: 1982-83, 1997-98, 2015-16) averaged +0.51 NAO in Jan-Feb against a climatological +0.16, with 33% of months negative against 39% normally. The tilt is smaller than the year-to-year spread (sd 0.98), and three events is not a forecastable sample. ENSO intensity does not tell you what the NAO will do this winter.",
      "what_does_hold": "The one robust feature is direction of change, not level: El Nino winters drift from a higher early-winter NAO toward a lower late-winter one, via a weakened stratospheric polar vortex, while neutral and La Nina winters drift the other way. That is a reason to prefer late-season trips in the southern Alps, not a reason to call the winter cold."
    }
  },
